      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We need to determine the number of different orders in which Jordan and his three best friends can run the first three laps, given that Jordan is fixed to run the fourth lap. This problem can be solved by counting the permutations of the three runners left (one runner per lap before the fourth).\n\nLet's denote the three runners as \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\). Jordan is fixed to run the fourth lap, which means we only need to arrange \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) in the first three positions. The number of ways to arrange \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) in three positions is given by the number of permutations of three distinct objects.\n\nThe number of permutations of three distinct objects is calculated as follows:\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\nSo, there are 6 different orders in which \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) can run the first three laps.\n\nTherefore, the number of different orders in which the four team members can run the race, with Jordan running the fourth lap, is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
